<title>GERMANY: Asian coffee production seen growing in 1996-2001.</title>
<headline>Asian coffee production seen growing in 1996-2001.</headline>
<dateline>HAMBURG 1996-09-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Coffee output in 14 Asian countries will rise to 21.18 million bags in the 2000/2001 season from 17.56 million in 1995/96, independent analyst Mick Wheeler said at the World Coffee Conference.</p>
<p>&quot;The outlook for the coffee industry in this region appears to be fairly bright and encouraging,&quot; he said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>Exportable output would rise to 16.18 milion bags from 13.10 million in the period under review.</p>
<p>Wheeler predicted output gains in China, Papua New Guinea, Indonesia, Vietnam and a number of other countries.</p>
<p>Indonesia was seen reaching a tentative eight million bag crop in 2000/2001 compared with 6.22 million in 1995/96. It could see bumper crops of up to nine million bags over the next few years if the weather was favourable.</p>
<p>Output in India and the Philippines should be flat to lower.</p>
<p>Wheeler provided the following table, based on personal estimates --</p>
<p>   Output in '000 bags	  1995/96	   2000/2001</p>
<p>   Burma			    40		    100</p>
<p>   Cambodia			  5		     20</p>
<p>   China			    80		    150</p>
<p>   India			 3,717		  3,700</p>
<p>   Indonesia		   6,218		  8,000</p>
<p>   Laos			    175		    300</p>
<p>   Malaysia			160		    200</p>
<p>   New Caledonia		   2			2</p>
<p>   Papua New Guinea	    997		  1,350</p>
<p>   Philippines		   890		    850</p>
<p>   Sri Lanka			50		     75</p>
<p>   Thailand		    1,264		  1,350</p>
<p>   Yemen			    60		     80</p>
<p>   Vietnam		     3,900		  5,000</p>
<p>   TOTAL			17,558		 21,177</p>
<p>--Vera Eckert, Hamburg newsroom +49-40-41903275</p>
